rcq("R07-D-08-4", "Application / analogy", "Difficult",
    "Which of the following productions would the author regard as making the fullest use of what the stage offers and the screen does not?",
    "A play in a mid-sized theatre whose cast is rehearsed to let the pacing of scenes follow the audience's reactions each night.",
    [("A play staged in a tiny room with spectators an arm's length from the actors, who are directed never to vary the timing of any line.",
      "This offers maximum proximity but suppresses responsiveness; the author says presence alone is 'close to a superstition' and that a production which suppresses responsiveness 'has given up the main thing that the stage offers'."),
     ("A play that projects close-ups of the actors' faces onto large screens so that every spectator can see the smallest expression.",
      "The close-up is named in paragraph 4 as one of film's 'resources of its own'; borrowing it does not use what is distinctive to the stage."),
     ("A play performed with such precision that critics who attend on different nights report seeing an identical performance.",
      "Paragraph 4 treats productions 'drilled to such precision that they vary little from night to night' as the case in which the live capacity goes unused.")],
    "The author locates the distinctive value of the stage in 'the performance and its audience' remaining 'capable of affecting one another' (paragraph 5), a capacity a production 'can choose to use ... or to suppress'. A cast trained to let pacing follow the house uses it most fully.",
    "Choosing the tiny-room production because it maximises presence, the argument the author rejects in paragraph 1.",
    "The tiny-room option matches the popular defence of theatre; rejecting it requires the passage's distinction between presence and responsiveness.",
    cognitive="Application", exams=("CAT", "XAT"))

